{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.AtomBranchConvexityBound","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.AtomBranchConvexityBound","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.BigOperators.Ring.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Positivity"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.atom_trace_bound_is_sharp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.atom_trace_bound_is_sharp","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/AtomBranchConvexityBound.lean#L78-L83","doc":"The bound is **sharp**: a one-branch expansion attains it.  Recorded so the\ntheorem above cannot be mistaken for a strict inequality, and so a future\nstrengthening has to confront the equality case. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.atom_trace_le_max_branch","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.atom_trace_le_max_branch","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/AtomBranchConvexityBound.lean#L68-L76","doc":"**The pruning consequence.**  The atom element's Markov trace is bounded by\nthe largest branch trace.  Since the bound is a single `max` over branches, the\nquestion \"can the atom carrier be large?\" reduces from `2^(2A+1)` independent\nquestions to one: *is any single branch large?* ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.abs_convex_combination_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.abs_convex_combination_le","kind":"theorem","line":48,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/AtomBranchConvexityBound.lean#L48-L66","doc":"**A convex combination cannot exceed its largest term.**\n\nIf the weights `w` are non-negative and sum to `1`, and every `t i` is bounded in\nabsolute value by `M`, then so is `∑ w i * t i`.  Stated for an arbitrary finite\nindex set, so it applies to the `2^(2A+1)` branch expansion without that\nexpansion being formalised. ","signatureChunk":0}]}